 * Myth — Ravens are aggressive birds
 * MYTH·20
 * Primary keyword: are ravens aggressive
 * Secondary: raven aggression, do ravens attack humans, raven dangerous
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Are Ravens Aggressive? Separating Myth from Raven Reality';
const description = 'Ravens can be defensive near nests and bold around food, but aggression toward humans is rare and contextual. This page examines the evidence for raven aggression, what actually triggers it, and why the \'dangerous bird\' reputation is largely unwarranted.';
const path        = '/myths/ravens-are-aggressive-birds/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',   url: '/' },
  { name: 'Myths',  url: '/myths/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Aggressive?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Will a raven attack a human?', a: 'Wild ravens will occasionally dive-bomb humans who approach their nest during breeding season (March–June), but actual physical contact is rare. The display is intimidatory: low, fast passes designed to drive the intruder away from the nest without direct confrontation. Outside nesting season, ravens rarely exhibit aggression toward humans. Habituated urban ravens (at campsites, landfills, tourist sites) may become bold around food — snatching items from hands or bags — but this is opportunism, not aggression in the predatory or defensive sense.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens dangerous?', a: 'Ravens are not dangerous to humans in any meaningful sense. A large raven weighs 800–1,600g — it is physically incapable of causing serious injury even in a worst-case nest-defence encounter. Their bill is powerful enough to cause a painful peck if grabbed, but ravens rarely make physical contact during nest-defence dives. The \'dangerous raven\' reputation comes from their size, black plumage, and historical cultural association with death rather than from documented harm to humans. In contrast, corvids much smaller than ravens (magpies, Australian magpies) are responsible for more documented human injuries during nesting season.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens aggressive to other birds?', a: 'Ravens are aggressively territorial toward other ravens in their breeding territory and will mob aerial predators (eagles, owls, buzzards) well away from the nest — an effective defensive strategy that also makes them useful early-warning systems for other birds. Ravens are dominant over most other species at carrion and food sources, displacing them through posture and size rather than active attack. Toward much larger animals (wolves, bears, humans), ravens use vocal alarm and dive-bombing rather than direct aggression. Among corvids, crows and jackdaws in groups may successfully mob individual ravens away from food.' },
];
